      <p class="eyebrow">About YAML</p>
      <h2 class="h-section mt-3">What is YAML, and why does it need formatting?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="space-y-6 text-zinc-600 leading-relaxed">
      <p>YAML (YAML Ain't Markup Language) is a human-readable data serialization format used for configuration files. Unlike JSON, which uses braces and quotes, YAML uses indentation to express structure — making it far easier to read and write by hand. That readability is why YAML is the default configuration language across the DevOps ecosystem.</p>

      <h3 class="h-sub text-zinc-900">Why YAML is so easy to break</h3>
      <p>YAML's readability comes at a price: it is extremely sensitive to whitespace. A tab instead of spaces, an inconsistent indentation level, or a missing space after a colon can cause a parse failure — or worse, a <em>silent</em> error where the parser reads your structure differently than you intended. Unlike a compiler, most YAML parsers give you only a vague message and no line number.</p>
      <p>That is exactly the problem a formatter solves. By normalizing indentation, aligning nesting, and validating syntax with precise error locations, it turns invisible whitespace bugs into something you can see and fix in seconds.</p>

      <h3 class="h-sub text-zinc-900">Where YAML is used</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Kubernetes</strong> — every resource (Deployment, Service, ConfigMap, Ingress) is defined in YAML.</li>
        <li><strong>Docker Compose</strong> — <code class="text-xs px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-zinc-100">docker-compose.yml</code> defines multi-container applications.</li>
        <li><strong>CI/CD</strong> — GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, and CircleCI all use YAML for workflow definitions.</li>
        <li><strong>Ansible</strong> — playbooks, roles, tasks, and inventories are written in YAML.</li>
        <li><strong>Configuration</strong> — Spring Boot, Helm charts, OpenAPI specs, and countless other tools rely on YAML.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="h-sub text-zinc-900">YAML vs JSON: which should you use?</h3>
      <p>YAML is a superset of JSON — every valid JSON document is also valid YAML. YAML adds comments and anchors (reusable blocks), which JSON lacks, making it the better choice for human-maintained configuration. JSON, however, parses faster and has unambiguous types, so it remains the standard for APIs and machine-to-machine data exchange. This is why YAML-to-JSON conversion is so common: you write config in readable YAML, then convert to JSON when a tool or API requires it. For a full comparison, see our <a href="#format-comparison" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline underline-offset-4">YAML vs JSON vs TOML comparison</a>.</p>
    </div>
